d2d1: Upload geometry through a reused dynamic buffer instead of new buffers per draw.

Drawing or filling a geometry created a D3D11 index buffer and a vertex
buffer with the primitive's data for each of its parts (lines, curves,
arcs), drew once and released them. Affinity draws hundreds of primitives
per frame on its canvas, so each frame created and destroyed thousands of
buffers: an allocation and an initial-data upload each on the
application's thread, and a buffer creation, upload and release each on
wined3d's.

Keep one dynamic buffer per device context, bound as both vertex and index
buffer. Each draw appends its indices and vertices with a single
NO_OVERWRITE map and draws from offsets; the buffer is discarded (renamed)
when it wraps, and grows when a draw does not fit.

The d2d1 conformance tests give the same result before and after (17491
tests, 0 failures, 245 todo). Measured in Affinity under a nested KWin on
an Arc A750, three fresh starts each:

  panning a vector document, CPU per presented frame:
      wined3d's command thread   7.4 ms -> 6.3 ms
      Affinity's UI thread       6.6 ms -> 5.7 ms
  brush tip behind the cursor (median of nine strokes):
                                 59 ms -> 51 ms

